Newspeak3
'Root'
class HopscotchForCroquet usingPlatform: p <Platform> hopscotch: H <HopscotchForHTML5 class> = Object new (
	(* Module definition enclosing Hopscotch UI subclass that integrates Croquet style synchronization. 
See that class (#Hopscotch) for details. *)
	
	(* imports *)
	
	|
		private HopscotchForHTML5 = H.
		private Promise = p actors Promise.
		private Resolver = p actors Resolver.
		private List = p collections List.
		private Map = p collections Map.
	|
) (
	public class Hopscotch usingPlatform: p <Platform> images: images ducts: ds voiceControlClass: VCC = HopscotchForHTML5 usingPlatform: p
		images: images
		ducts: ds
		voiceControlClass: VCC (
		(* This class overrides the normal Hopscotch UI module to allow the UI to synchronize using croquet.io. A series of croquet clients running a Newspeak application can be synchronized, without any modification to the Newspeak application code; the only requirement is that application be deployed using a platform object that uses an instance of this class as its GUI.

For every interactive fragment class, we maintain:

1) A subclass, defined here, that manages the interaction with Croquet.
2) A counter that is used to generate unique ids for each fragment.

Each fragment sets its id upon instantiation, accessing and incrementing the per-fragment type counter via a dedicated method in this class.

The fragment notifies the Croquet root view when an interaction occurs. The croquet view notifies the croquet model, which notifies the reflector; when the reflector has signaled the croquet model, it notifies the croquet view; the view then executes a call back in which the fragment performs the normal behavior defined in its superclass.

As a general recipe:

Every fragment type has a per type croquet event scope id, of the form #ns<fragmentypename>_, and, for each of its actions, a croquet event.  When an action occurs, we publish an event with the fragment type scope id, the action-specific event name, and event specific data. Usually this data is  a JS object with two fields: fid, containing the individual fragment id publishing, and a data field for additional event info. We produce these objects by calling out to JS via #newspeakFragmentData:data:. This JS function takes a fragment id and a data object, which can be anything that Croquet can serialize (so numbers, strings or vanilla objects, but not instances of specific classes with function valued fields etc.).

Back in Javascript, the root model subscribes to these events , scoping them using the aforementioned fragment type scope id.  The subscription specifies a root model method. When an event is detected, the method will be called with the event data as a parameter. The method in turn publishes an event whose name is of the form #model_<fragment specific scope>_ <action>. The fragment specific scope is the concatenation of the fragment type scope id and a fragment id number, which is usually found in the fid field of the incoming method argument. Associated with the event is the data from the incoming parameter.

Each fragment subscribes to such an event for each of its actions. Thus, it will be notified when croquet has processed the action - regardless of whether the action originated on the client that actually published, or any other client. Now it can act on the event data it receives.

The scheme is designed so that we only generate events upon user interaction. Thus the event pressure is bounded by the speed of human input. 

A complication to the above scheme is Croquet's use of snapshotting. Croquet expects all application state to be stored as serializable data in a Javascript Croquet model object, with the UI managed by the Croquet view objects that are essentially stateless. We are of course subverting this model, as all our state is in the Newspeak heap. In principle, we could add an image mechanism to Newspeak, store that image as a blob in the Croquet model and use it to restore the system. For now, we use a different solution. We store the entire event history in the model. This is a legitimate representation of the application state, as it can always be reconstructed from its initial state (the vfuel) by applying all events in order. 

When restoring a client from a Croquet snapshot, we play back any events in the snapshot that have not yet been seen by the client, bringing into sync. The logic managing the above process is located mainly in Javascript code. When the client starts up, it sets up Croquet before activating Newspeak. Usually, this will include playing back unprocessed events.  However, in the case where a fresh client starts up, Newspeak has not yet been run, no Hopscotch fragments have been created, and so no fragments have subscribed to events.  Hence, replaying events would have no effect in a fresh client. To remedy this, we have Hopscotch notify the JS code to replay after the very first presenter has been displayed. This is managed in #HopscotchShell>>displayPresenter:. We track whether this is the first presenter via the flag #isStartingUp.

A few other details also complicate the picture. These have to do with file handling, drag and drop, and menus, and are described in classes #FileChooserFragment, #MediaCreatorFragment and #Menu respectively.

Ultimately, we hope to go beyond the above, and be able to diff the saved model state and extend this to full local-first solution. *)
		
		(* Per fragment-type counters for managing fragment ids *)
		
		(* Fragment numbering is SCOPED. A fragment's id is its enclosing scope path
     plus an ordinal within that scope, and that id is the Croquet event address,
     so clients agree on what an event refers to only while they mint ids the same
     way. A flat counter made that depend on global construction ORDER, which
     deferred, async and lazily realized content each break. See
     CROQUET_SCOPED_FRAGMENT_IDS_2026-08-18.md. *)
		
		|
			fragmentScopePath <String> ::= ''.
			scopedCounters <Map[String, Integer]> = Map new.
			liveFileChoosers = List new.
			isStartingUp ::= true.
			
			(* JS access *)
			js = p js.
		|
	) (
		public class ButtonFragment label: l action: a = super ButtonFragment label: l
			action: a (
			|
				public fragmentId <String> ::= newButtonId.
				scope_slot <String>
			|
		) (
			croquetEventSpecs ^<Array[Symbol]> = (
				^{#model_button_click. }
			)
			createVisual ^<Alien[ButtonElement]> = (
				ensureCroquetSubscriptions.
				^super createVisual
			)
			updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment <ButtonFragment> ^<Alien[ButtonElement]> = (
				adoptCroquetIdentityFrom: oldFragment.
				^super updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment
			)
			action = (
				^[ publish: 'nsbutton_' event: #button_click data: fragmentId ]
			)
			public scope ^<String> = (
				(* Lazily return a unique identifier for this fragment *)
				
				scope_slot isNil ifTrue: [ scope_slot: 'nsbutton_' , fragmentId ].
				^scope_slot
			)
			setupCroquetView = (
				(* When the Croquet view is notified of a click, it will call back to the Newspeak button to perform its normal action *)
				
				subscribe: #model_button_click handler: [:e | super action value ]
			)
			croquetLabelDetail ^<String> = (
				(* The button's text, when it is text: Apply and Cancel are siblings of one kind in one presenter, and only this tells them apart in the alarm's label check. *)
				
				^label isKindOfString ifTrue: [ ':' , label ] ifFalse: [ '' ]
			)
		) : ()
		public class ImageButtonFragment new = super ImageButtonFragment new (
			|
				public fragmentId <String> ::= newImageButtonId.
				scope_slot <String>
			|
		) (
			croquetEventSpecs ^<Array[Symbol]> = (
				^{#model_image_button_click. }
			)
			createVisual ^<Alien[Element]> = (
				ensureCroquetSubscriptions.
				^super createVisual
			)
			public scope ^<String> = (
				scope_slot isNil ifTrue: [ scope_slot: 'nsImagebutton_' , fragmentId ].
				^scope_slot
			)
			updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment <ImageButtonFragment> ^<Alien[ButtonElement]> = (
				adoptCroquetIdentityFrom: oldFragment.
				^super updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment
			)
			action = (
				^[ publish: 'nsImagebutton_' event: #image_button_click data: fragmentId ]
			)
			setupCroquetView = (
				subscribe: #model_image_button_click handler: [:e | super action value ]
			)
		) : ()
		public class CheckboxFragment text: t <Holder> value: v <Boolean | Holder> action: a <[:Boolean | Object]> = super CheckboxFragment text: t
			value: v
			action: a (
			|
				public fragmentId <String> ::= newCheckboxId.
				scope_slot <String>
			|
		) (
			croquetEventSpecs ^<Array[Symbol]> = (
				^{#model_checkBox_checked. #model_checkBox_unchecked. }
			)
			createVisual ^<Alien[Element]> = (
				ensureCroquetSubscriptions.
				^super createVisual
			)
			public scope ^<String> = (
				scope_slot isNil ifTrue: [ scope_slot: 'nscheckbox_' , fragmentId ].
				^scope_slot
			)
			updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment <CheckboxFragment> ^<Alien[ButtonElement]> = (
				adoptCroquetIdentityFrom: oldFragment.
				^super updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment
			)
			writeInput: v = (
				checkbox at: 'checked' put: v.
				super writeInput: v
			)
			inputHandler ^<[:Alien | Boolean]> = (
				^[:b <Boolean> |
					(checkbox at: 'checked') printString out.
					publish: 'nscheckbox_'
						event: ((checkbox at: 'checked') ifTrue: [ #checkBox_unchecked ]
							ifFalse: [ #checkBox_checked ])
						data: fragmentId.
					nil
				]
			)
			setupCroquetView = (
				subscribe: #model_checkBox_checked
					handler: [:e |
						'checkbox checked event ' out.
						writeInput: true
					].
				subscribe: #model_checkBox_unchecked
					handler: [:e |
						'checkbox unchecked event ' out.
						writeInput: false
					]
			)
		) : ()
		public class RadioButtonFragment text: t <String> value: v <Boolean | Holder> group: g <String> action: a <[:Boolean | Object]> = super RadioButtonFragment text: t
			value: v
			group: g
			action: a (
			|
				public fragmentId <String> ::= newRadioButtonId.
				scope_slot <String>
			|
		) (
			croquetEventSpecs ^<Array[Symbol]> = (
				^{#model_radioButton_pressed. #model_radioButton_released. }
			)
			createVisual ^<Alien[Element]> = (
				ensureCroquetSubscriptions.
				^super createVisual
			)
			public scope ^<String> = (
				scope_slot isNil ifTrue: [ scope_slot: 'nsradiobutton_' , fragmentId ].
				^scope_slot
			)
			updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment <RadioButtonFragment> ^<Alien[ButtonElement]> = (
				adoptCroquetIdentityFrom: oldFragment.
				^super updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment
			)
			writeInput: v = (
				button at: 'checked' put: v.
				super writeInput: v
			)
			inputHandler ^<[:Alien | Boolean]> = (
				^[:b <Boolean> |
					publish: 'nsradiobutton_'
						event: ((button at: 'checked') ifTrue: [ #radioButton_pressed ]
							ifFalse: [ #radioButton_released ])
						data: fragmentId.
					nil
				]
			)
			setupCroquetView = (
				subscribe: #model_radioButton_pressed handler: [:e | writeInput: true ].
				subscribe: #model_radioButton_released handler: [:e | writeInput: false ]
			)
		) : ()
		public class HyperlinkFragment label: l action: a = super HyperlinkFragment label: l
			action: a (
			|
				public fragmentId <String> ::= newHyperlinkId.
				scope_slot <String>
			|
		) (
			croquetEventSpecs ^<Array[Symbol]> = (
				^{#model_hyperlink_click. }
			)
			createVisual ^<Alien[Element]> = (
				ensureCroquetSubscriptions.
				^super createVisual
			)
			updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment <HyperlinkFragment> ^<Alien[LinkElement]> = (
				adoptCroquetIdentityFrom: oldFragment.
				^super updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment
			)
			action = (
				^[ publish: 'nshyperlink_' event: #hyperlink_click data: fragmentId ]
			)
			public scope ^<String> = (
				scope_slot isNil ifTrue: [ scope_slot: 'nshyperlink_' , fragmentId ].
				^scope_slot
			)
			setupCroquetView = (
				subscribe: #model_hyperlink_click handler: [:e | super action value ]
			)
			croquetLabelDetail ^<String> = (
				(* The link's text, when it is text - as for buttons: what tells sibling links of one presenter apart in the alarm's label check. *)
				
				^label isKindOfString ifTrue: [ ':' , label ] ifFalse: [ '' ]
			)
		) : ()
		public class CodeMirrorFragment onText: t <String> = super CodeMirrorFragment onText: t (
			|
				public fragmentId <String> ::= newCodeMirrorId.
				scope_slot <String>
				reflecting <Boolean> ::= true.
				
				(* Coalesced publishing of user edits; see respondToChange:. pendingChange is the latest user change not yet published (nil when there is none), changeFlushTimer the JS timer that will publish it (nil when none is set), lastChangeSeq the ordinal of this client's latest published change for this editor. All three start out nil, deliberately: an editor that was live when this code was hot-loaded has them as nil too. *)
				pendingChange <Alien[JSObject]>
				changeFlushTimer <Alien>
				lastChangeSeq <Integer>
			|
		) (
			croquetEventSpecs ^<Array[Symbol]> = (
				^{#model_codeMirror_change. #model_codeMirror_beforeChange. #model_codeMirror_keydown. #model_codeMirror_accept. #model_codeMirror_cancel. #model_codeMirror_beforeSelectionChange. }
			)
			createVisual ^<Alien[Element]> = (
				ensureCroquetSubscriptions.
				^super createVisual
			)
			public scope ^<String> = (
				scope_slot isNil ifTrue: [ scope_slot: 'nscodemirror_' , fragmentId ].
				^scope_slot
			)
			preventDefault: event <Alien> = (
				(* Only the initiating client has an event to manage, and others have no access to this event; it cannot be serialized, so cannot be passed via croquet to other clients, and it would not be a valid event if it was. *)
				
			)
			respondToCancel = (
				reflecting ifTrue: [
						
						(* A cancel reverts the text, so an edit still waiting to be published is dropped, not flushed: published after the cancel, it would put every client back into the edit state with the text just abandoned. *)
						discardPendingChange.
						publish: 'nscodemirror_' event: #codeMirror_cancel data: codeMirrorData
					]
			)
			acceptFromKeydown: event <Alien> = (
				super respondToAccept: event
			)
			convertKeydown: kde <Alien[KeydownEvent]> ^<Alien[JSObject]> = (
				^js global sanitizeKeydownEvent: kde
			)
			cancelFromKeydown = (
				super respondToCancel
			)
			codeMirrorData = (
				^js global newspeakFragmentData: fragmentId data: textBeingAccepted
			)
			codeMirrorData: change <Alien[CodeMirrorChangeObject]> = (
				^js global newspeakFragmentData: fragmentId
					data: (js global nsCodeMirrorData: textBeingAccepted change: change)
			)
			croquetOrigin ^<Alien[JSObject]> = (
				(* Options object tagging a programmatic CodeMirror operation as caused by synchronization, so the publish hooks can recognize and suppress its signals. The reflecting flag alone cannot: CodeMirror delivers some signals (beforeSelectionChange from a cursor adjustment above all) only when its operation or display update ends - AFTER the flag has been restored - and the resulting echo of every applied event stormed the reflector past its send rate limit; each disconnect's replay then re-fired the echoes en masse, a self-sustaining storm.
      Also scroll: false - by default CodeMirror scrolls its cursor into view on setCursor/setSelection, so applying an echo to a VISIBLE editor yanked the page to that editor's cursor. Bit hardest when typing in a document's live view with the raw view open: every keystroke's echo scrolled to the raw pane's insertion point, hiding what was being typed. A synchronization-applied cursor must never steal the viewport. *)
				
				| opts = JSObject new. |
				opts at: #origin put: 'croquet'.
				opts at: #scroll put: false.
				^opts
			)
			adjustCursorPos: change <Alien[CodeMirrorChangeRecord]> = (
				(* The change record from CodeMirror will have information on:

indices of change (#from, #to fields)
new text (#text field - an array of lines of new text)
old text (#removed field) *)
				
				| text = change at: #text. linesInserted = text at: #length. from = change at: #from. fromLine = from at: #line. toLine = (change at: #to) at: #line. linesReplaced = toLine - fromLine + 1. linesAdded = linesInserted - linesReplaced. targetLine = toLine + linesAdded. lastLineLength = linesInserted = 0 ifTrue: [
						0
					]
					ifFalse: [ (text at: linesInserted - 1) size ]. baseCh = fromLine = targetLine ifTrue: [
						from at: #ch
					]
					ifFalse: [ 0 ]. targetCh = baseCh + lastLineLength. |
				editor setCursor: (js global nsCursorPos: targetCh line: targetLine)
					ch: nil
					options: croquetOrigin
			)
			respondToKeyDown: event <Alien[Event]> = (
				| key <Character> = event at: 'key'. metaPressed <Boolean> = 
				(* customary on mac, does not work on windows *)
				event at: 'metaKey'. ctrlPressed <Boolean> = 
				(* works on windows *)
				event at: 'ctrlKey'. shiftPressed <Boolean> = event at: 'shiftKey'. |
				
				(* Only the keys the base response acts on are published: a modified Enter (accept with meta or ctrl, evaluate with shift) and Escape (cancel). For every other key the base method falls through with no effect, so its event bought nothing - and it was one of three per keystroke (with beforeChange and change), against a reflector budget of about 20 messages a second. The edit-state conditions are left to the base response, which evaluates them on each client at dispatch, over replicated state. If the base response ever acts on another key, this test must admit it. *)
				reflecting ifFalse: [ ^self ].
				((key = 'Enter' and: [ metaPressed or: [ ctrlPressed or: [ shiftPressed ] ] ]) or: [
						key = 'Escape'
					]) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
				event preventDefault.
				
				(* Accept and evaluate read each client's own editor, so the text must get there first. *)
				flushPendingChange.
				publish: 'nscodemirror_'
					event: #codeMirror_keydown
					data: (js global newspeakFragmentData: fragmentId
						data: (convertKeydown: event))
			)
			respondToAccept: event <Alien[Event]> = (
				reflecting ifTrue: [
						
						(* The accept response reads each client's own editor, so the text must get there first. *)
						flushPendingChange.
						publish: 'nscodemirror_' event: #codeMirror_accept data: codeMirrorData
					]
			)
			selectionEventData: event = (
				^js global nsCodeMirrorSelectionChange: event
			)
			adjustSelection: selection <Alien[Range]> = (
				editor setSelection: (selection at: #anchor)
					head: (selection at: #head)
					options: croquetOrigin
			)
			isSyncOrigin: origin ^<Boolean> = (
				(* Selection/cursor signals originating from applying a synchronized event: our tagged programmatic operations ('croquet') and the selection fallout of a full-buffer setValue. CodeMirror may deliver these at the END of its operation - after the reflecting flag has been restored - so the flag alone cannot suppress their echo, which stormed the reflector (see croquetOrigin). The change/beforeChange publishers consult it too, through isUserOrigin:: a setValue-originated change is handled locally rather than published. (It used to be published so that the change response would run on the echo; the response now runs directly, on every client, from its own re-render.) *)
				
				origin = 'croquet' ifTrue: [ ^true ].
				origin = 'setValue' ifTrue: [ ^true ].
				^false
			)
			respondToBeforeSelectionChange: event <Alien[Event]> = (
				(* Published only for a selection the USER moved ('*mouse', '+move',
		   the selection that accompanies '+input' ...). A programmatic
		   setCursor/setSelection, tagged or not, is every client's own
		   business, exactly as a programmatic change is - see isUserOrigin:. *)
				
				(reflecting and: [ isUserOrigin: (event at: #origin) ]) ifTrue: [
						publish: 'nscodemirror_'
							event: #codeMirror_beforeSelectionChange
							data: (js global newspeakFragmentData: fragmentId
								data: (selectionEventData: event))
					]
			)
			updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment <CodeMirrorFragment> ^<Alien[Element]> = (
				(* The reflecting flag is deliberately NOT copied from the old fragment: its steady-state value is true, and a mid-application false belongs to the old fragment's handler, whose epilogue restores the OLD instance's flag - copying false here would leave nobody to restore ours, and the next incoming event would be swallowed. Echo fallout of an in-flight application is suppressed by origin tags (isSyncOrigin:), not by the flag. *)
				
				adoptCroquetIdentityFrom: oldFragment.
				adoptChangeStateFrom: oldFragment.
				^super updateVisualsFromSameKind: oldFragment
			)
			adoptChangeStateFrom: oldFragment <CodeMirrorFragment> = (
				(* The editor passes to us (see the base updateVisualsFromSameKind:), and with it any user edit the old fragment had not yet published and the ordinal of its latest published change, so that an echo still on its way is recognized here. The old fragment's timer is cancelled; ours takes over. *)
				
				| state <{Alien[JSObject]. Integer}> = oldFragment releaseChangeState. |
				lastChangeSeq:: state last.
				state first isNil ifFalse: [
						pendingChange:: state first.
						scheduleChangeFlush
					]
			)
			public releaseChangeState ^<{Alien[JSObject]. Integer}> = (
				(* See adoptChangeStateFrom:. Public, because the adopting fragment is another object. *)
				
				| state = {pendingChange. publishedChangeSeq. }. |
				discardPendingChange.
				^state
			)
			updateEditedText: oldFragment <CodeMirrorFragment> = (
				(* We don't want spurious changes signaling croquet, so we update only when needed. This isn't quite right. This will update when needed, but even that is not a real change. This method is called when no edits have occurred. The situation to worry about is when we edited a method and changed the selector, changing a different method. That method has changed, even though it was not being edited. At the same time, the edited method has not changed, yet the text in its editor has. So it was always best to just update the method. However, we do need to avoid the change events thereby generated. Once the correct test for this is implemented, it will likely be cheaper to use it to avoid the notification than to compare old and new text. *)
				
				(* The caller (the base updateVisualsFromSameKind:) raises
		   lastChangeWasSynthetic before calling here, counting on the setValue
		   to fire the change that consumes it. When the text is unchanged there
		   is no setValue and no change, so lower the flag ourselves: left raised
		   it eats the NEXT real change, and a paste - a single change - then
		   never enters edit state and the accept/cancel bar never appears. *)
				
				textSlot = oldFragment textBeingAccepted ifTrue: [
						lastChangeWasSynthetic:: false
					]
					ifFalse: [ editor setValue: textSlot ]
			)
			setupCroquetView = (
				subscribe: #model_codeMirror_change
					handler: [:e |
						reflecting ifTrue: [
								| ahead <Boolean> localText <String> localCursor <Alien> |
								
								(* ahead: this is an echo of our own change, and the user has typed past it (see isBehindLocalEdits:). Every client must still run the response over the event's text - the response may read the editor, and clients that ran it over different texts would part ways - so the echo is applied here as everywhere; what the user typed since is put back afterwards, in the same turn, so nothing is painted in between. *)
								ahead:: hasVisual and: [ isBehindLocalEdits: e ].
								ahead ifTrue: [
										localText:: editor getValue.
										localCursor:: editor getCursor
									].
								reflecting:: false.
								
								(* The before-change response rides this event (see respondToBeforeChange:). It runs first, ahead of the text, as it did when it had an event of its own. *)
								super respondToBeforeChange: e.
								hasVisual ifTrue: [
										| newText = e at: #textBeingAccepted. |
										
										(* An editor that already holds the text - the typist's, once it has caught up - is left alone: setValue would move its cursor and selection for nothing. *)
										editor getValue = newText ifFalse: [
												editor setValue: newText.
												adjustCursorPos: (e at: #change)
											]
									].
								super respondToChange: e.
								ahead ifTrue: [
										editor setValue: localText.
										editor setCursor: localCursor ch: nil options: croquetOrigin
									].
								reflecting:: true
							]
							ifFalse: [ reflecting:: true ]
					].
				subscribe: #model_codeMirror_beforeChange
					handler: [:e |
						reflecting ifTrue: [
								reflecting:: false.
								super respondToBeforeChange: e.
								reflecting:: true
							]
							ifFalse: [ reflecting:: true ]
					].
				subscribe: #model_codeMirror_keydown
					handler: [:e |
						reflecting ifTrue: [
								reflecting:: false.
								super respondToKeyDown: e.
								reflecting:: true
							]
							ifFalse: [ reflecting:: true ]
					].
				subscribe: #model_codeMirror_accept
					handler: [:e |
						reflecting ifTrue: [
								reflecting:: false.
								super respondToAccept: e.
								reflecting:: true
							]
							ifFalse: [ reflecting:: true ]
					].
				subscribe: #model_codeMirror_cancel
					handler: [:e |
						reflecting ifTrue: [
								reflecting:: false.
								super respondToCancel.
								reflecting:: true
							]
							ifFalse: [ reflecting:: true ]
					].
				subscribe: #model_codeMirror_beforeSelectionChange
					handler: [:e |
						reflecting ifTrue: [
								reflecting:: false.
								adjustSelection: e.
								reflecting:: true
							]
							ifFalse: [ reflecting:: true ]
					]
			)
			respondToChange: change <Alien[CodeMirrorChangeObject]> = (
				(* reflecting is false while a synchronized event is being applied; its
		   handler (setupCroquetView) runs the base response itself, so nothing
		   to do here. Otherwise: a USER edit is published, and its response runs
		   when the event comes back from the model, on every client alike. A
		   PROGRAMMATIC change - a setValue from a re-render, or one of our own
		   tagged operations - is a deterministic consequence of replicated state
		   that every client produces for itself, in lockstep; it must not enter
		   the stream. Its response runs locally, right here. See isUserOrigin:. *)
				
				reflecting ifFalse: [ ^self ].
				(isUserOrigin: (change at: #origin)) ifFalse: [
						^super respondToChange: change
					].
				
				(* Published at most once per changeCoalescingWindow, not once per keystroke: the event carries the editor's full text, so the changes in between are redundant, and the reflector drops a client that exceeds about 20 messages a second. The first unpublished change starts the timer and later ones do not restart it, so the delay is bounded by the window however fast the typing. *)
				pendingChange:: js global nsCodeMirrorChange: change.
				scheduleChangeFlush
			)
			changeCoalescingWindow ^<Integer> = (
				(* Milliseconds. The most a user edit waits to be published, and so the least time between two change events from one editor. To be tuned by feel. *)
				
				^80
			)
			scheduleChangeFlush = (
				changeFlushTimer isNil ifFalse: [ ^self ].
				changeFlushTimer:: js global setTimeout: [
						[
							changeFlushTimer:: nil.
							flushPendingChange
						] on: Exception do: [:ex | nil ].
						nil
					]
					with: changeCoalescingWindow
			)
			flushPendingChange = (
				(* Publish the pending user edit now, if there is one: the editor's text as it stands, with the latest change record (which places the cursor on the other clients). Sent by the timer, and ahead of anything that reads the other clients' editors (accept, evaluate). The event names its sender and ordinal so that the sender can tell, when it comes back, whether the user has typed past it (isBehindLocalEdits:). *)
				
				| change <Alien[JSObject]> data <Alien[JSObject]> |
				pendingChange isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
				change:: pendingChange.
				discardPendingChange.
				hasVisual ifFalse: [ ^self ].
				lastChangeSeq:: publishedChangeSeq + 1.
				data:: js global nsCodeMirrorData: textBeingAccepted change: change.
				data at: #sender put: localViewId.
				data at: #seq put: lastChangeSeq.
				publish: 'nscodemirror_'
					event: #codeMirror_change
					data: (js global newspeakFragmentData: fragmentId data: data)
			)
			discardPendingChange = (
				pendingChange:: nil.
				changeFlushTimer isNil ifFalse: [
						js global clearTimeout: changeFlushTimer.
						changeFlushTimer:: nil
					]
			)
			publishedChangeSeq ^<Integer> = (
				^lastChangeSeq isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ lastChangeSeq ]
			)
			localViewId ^<String> = (
				(* This client's identity in the session. A replaying joiner's is fresh, so no recorded event is ever its own. *)
				
				^rootView at: #viewId
			)
			isBehindLocalEdits: e <Alien[JSObject]> ^<Boolean> = (
				(* Whether e is this client's own change coming back while the user has typed past it: an edit is waiting to be published, or a later one is already on its way. Only then may the local text be kept over the event's (see setupCroquetView) - a further change of ours is then certain to follow and bring every client, this one included, to the same text. An event recorded before changes named their sender answers false. *)
				
				(e at: #sender) = localViewId ifFalse: [ ^false ].
				pendingChange isNil ifFalse: [ ^true ].
				^(e at: #seq) < publishedChangeSeq
			)
			respondToBeforeChange: event <Alien[Event]> = (
				(* As respondToChange:, except that a USER edit publishes nothing here. The base response is updateEditState and nothing overrides it, so an event of its own - carrying the editor's full text - bought one call per client. That call is now made by the handler of the change event that always follows (setupCroquetView), immediately before the change response: the same calls in the same order, for one published event per keystroke instead of two and half the bytes. A change CodeMirror cancels in beforeChange would leave the call unmade; nothing here cancels one.

The model_codeMirror_beforeChange subscription stays, for sessions recorded while the event was still published. *)
				
				reflecting ifFalse: [ ^self ].
				(isUserOrigin: (event at: #origin)) ifFalse: [
						^super respondToBeforeChange: event
					]
			)
			isUserOrigin: origin ^<Boolean> = (
				(* True for a change the user made in THIS editor - CodeMirror tags
		   those '+input', '+delete', 'paste', 'cut', 'undo', ... - which is what
		   the other clients need told about. False for 'setValue' and 'croquet'
		   (see isSyncOrigin:) and for an untagged programmatic operation, whose
		   origin is undefined.

		   Why this matters: programmatic changes used to be published too, so
		   that their change response would run when the event came back. But
		   every client re-renders the same way and published the same setValue,
		   multiplying each one by the number of clients; and a late joiner,
		   re-rendering as it replayed, published its own on top of the recorded
		   ones - 48 extra events and five extra editors in one probe run
		   (croquet-probes/setup-latejoin-probe.js, 2026-09-12). Those echoes drove
		   extra change responses and re-renders, and so extra fragment
		   ordinals, on the joiner alone: every later keystroke then addressed an
		   editor the joiner had numbered differently, and its replay stalled
		   15s per event. *)
				
				origin isKindOfString ifFalse: [ ^false ].
				^(isSyncOrigin: origin) not
			)
		) : ()
